{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Say to them.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: ״אֲלֵהֶם״ — these are those who stood before Mount Sinai, end quote. It was difficult for them [to explain] the superfluity of ״אֱמֹר אֲלֵהֶם״: if it refers to those mentioned in the preceding [passage], who are the priests, there was no need to say it, for it was to them that He began to speak, and this is the conclusion of the commandment about which He had begun to speak. And if it refers to Israel — behold, they were not mentioned in the proximity of this! He said [it refers] to those who stood at Mount Sinai, for there the nation was united with its Elohim, and when He speaks without specification, the matter is understood to be about them. Accordingly, this punishment applies also to Israel. And if you say, if so, why throughout the entire Torah does He mention בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל at each and every particular, and it did not suffice to say ״דַּבֵּר אֲלֵהֶם״? They of blessed memory already said (Leviticus Rabbah 2:5): a parable of one who had a beloved son, and his remembrance was constantly in his mouth — ״Eat, my son,״ ״Drink, my son.״ So too, the Holy One, blessed be He — out of His love for Israel, their remembrance is sweet upon Him in His mouth. And in a place where there is something to expound, we expound."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:10.762408111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}